<Layouts.marketing current_path={assigns[:current_path]} flash={@flash} current_scope={@current_scope} page_title={@page_title}>
  <%!-- ───────────── HERO — the money passage ─────────────
        First two sentences are the liftable "what is CodeMySpec" answer. A
        retrieval system pulls this passage, so it has to stand alone. --%>
  <section class="bg-base-200 border-b border-base-300">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 lg:py-24 grid lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,1.05fr)] gap-12 lg:gap-14 items-center">
      <div>
      <div class="badge badge-primary badge-outline gap-2 mb-6">
        <span class="inline-block w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-primary"></span>
        Free to build · your model, your keys · no lock-in
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl font-bold tracking-tight leading-[1.05] max-w-4xl">
        AI can build you an app in an hour.<br />
        <span class="text-primary">Then you change one thing, and it falls down.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-base-content/70 mt-6 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-base-content">
          CodeMySpec is for the morning after. Build software that stays reliable without writing code
          and without prompting the model.
        </strong>
        You work through a process instead: say what you want, agree what it should do, and let the
        harness hold the machine to it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base-content/70 mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        <span class="font-semibold text-base-content">You don't supervise it. The harness supervises it.</span>
        You bring your own AI account, nothing traps your work, and building is free.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4">
        <.link navigate={~p"/install"} class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Install the plugin — free</.link>
        <a href="#specs" class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg">How the specs work</a>
      </div>
      </div>

      <%!-- No code samples on a product page. The scenario is the readable half of
           an executable specification — plain language anyone can check — and the
           passing state is what makes "the specifications execute" concrete. --%>
      <figure class="min-w-0">
        <div class="card bg-base-100 border border-base-300 shadow-sm overflow-hidden">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3 px-5 py-3 border-b border-base-300 bg-base-200">
            <span class="text-sm font-semibold">Card lock</span>
            <span class="text-xs text-base-content/50 whitespace-nowrap">
              story 412 · criterion 3180
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 space-y-4">
            <div class="flex gap-4">
              <span class="w-16 shrink-0 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-primary pt-0.5">
                Given
              </span>
              <span class="text-base-content/85">an active card</span>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4">
              <span class="w-16 shrink-0 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-primary pt-0.5">
                When
              </span>
              <span class="text-base-content/85">the owner locks it</span>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4">
              <span class="w-16 shrink-0 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-primary pt-0.5">
                Then
              </span>
              <span class="text-base-content/85">the next swipe is declined</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5 px-5 py-3 border-t border-base-300 bg-primary/5">
            <span class="hero-check-circle-mini size-5 text-primary shrink-0"></span>
            <span class="text-sm font-medium text-primary">Verified against your code</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/60 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
          Behavior you agreed on, in plain language, tied to the story it came from. This is a
          specification that runs, so it either holds or it does not.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- ───────────── THE WORKDAY ─────────────
        The most persuasive thing available to a non-technical reader: it turns
        every feature into a shape they can picture — a day, a rhythm, and sleep.
        Source: the operator narrative (SOURCE-operator-narrative.md). --%>
  <section class="border-b border-base-300">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <.eyebrow name="what a day looks like" />
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        A workday with a beginning and an end.
      </h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-base-content/70 mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-base-content">
          You take in five stories in the morning, it builds all day, you try it in the evening, and
          it fixes what you found overnight.
        </strong>
        Then you take the next five. Most projects come out to about ten of these, roughly a business
        day each.
      </p>

      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4 mt-12">
        <div :for={
          {when_, what, who} <- [
            {"Morning", "Take in five stories and answer the design questions.", "you"},
            {"All day", "It builds. You are not in the room for this part.", "it"},
            {"Evening", "You click around like a real person and say what's wrong.", "you"},
            {"Overnight", "It fixes what you found. Come back to a working app.", "it"}
          ]
        } class={[
          "card border shadow-sm",
          who == "you" && "bg-base-100 border-base-300 border-t-4 border-t-primary",
          who == "it" && "bg-base-200 border-base-300 border-t-4 border-t-base-300"
        ]}>
          <div class="card-body">
            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2">
              <span class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-primary">
                {when_}
              </span>
              <span class="text-[10px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-base-content/40">
                {who}
              </span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-base-content/80 mt-2 leading-relaxed">{what}</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-2xl space-y-4 text-base-content/70 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          <span class="font-semibold text-base-content">Start smaller than you want to.</span>
          Five stories, not fifty — a core you can hold in your head and get running in a day. That
          limit is for your benefit, not the machine's. If you already drew the whole thing, that work
          isn't wasted; it just isn't the first move.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- ───────────── THE KEYSTONE — executable specs ─────────────
        This is the load-bearing section. Written specs are the optional layer
        (require_specs is a config knob); the executable specification is the
        mandatory one, and it is what makes the harness work. --%>
  <section id="specs" class="border-b border-base-300 bg-base-200">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <.eyebrow name="the core innovation" />
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        If your application is the castle, <span class="text-primary">the BDD specs are the walls.</span>
      </h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-base-content/70 mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-base-content">
          Every rule you agree to becomes a test that uses your application the way a person would.
        </strong>
        Given someone who has never signed up, when they sign up, the terms appear. The test clicks the
        actual button and checks for the actual words on the actual screen. So the machine can't write
        itself a fake test that passes and move on. It has to make the thing work the way you would
        find it working.
      </p>

      <p class="text-base-content/70 mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        Because they run, they can't quietly drift from the code, and they can't rot into
        documentation nobody trusts.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-sm">
        <.link navigate={~p"/blog/bdd-specs-for-ai-generated-code"} class="link link-primary font-medium">
          How BDD specs catch what unit tests confirm →
        </.link>
        <.link navigate={~p"/blog/bdd-attention-three-amigos"} class="link link-primary font-medium">
          Where the scenarios come from →
        </.link>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- ───────────── THE PROCESS (diagram) ─────────────
        Five steps from the user's side, not the seven-node engine graph. The
        you/it marking is the value proposition made visible: the reader never
        appears in a step that involves writing code or prompting a model. --%>
  <section id="loop" class="border-b border-base-300">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <.eyebrow name="how it works" />
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        You work the process. It does the labor.
      </h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-base-content/70 mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-base-content">
          Five steps, the same five every time, and only two of them are yours.
        </strong>
        You say what you want and whether it came out right. Everything between those
        two is the machine's problem, and the harness is what holds it to the job.
      </p>

      <%!-- Theme-aware via currentColor; scrolls in its own container on narrow
           screens rather than squashing to illegibility. --%>
      <figure class="mt-12">
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-base-300 bg-base-100">
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 1040 250"
            class="w-full h-auto min-w-[820px]"
            role="img"
            aria-labelledby="loop-title loop-desc"
          >
            <title id="loop-title">The CodeMySpec process</title>
            <desc id="loop-desc">
              Five steps in order: plan, spec, code, QA, and review. You do the planning, the
              specification and the final review; the machine writes the code and QAs the running
              application. Anything QA finds goes back to the code step before you ever see it.
            </desc>

            <g class="text-base-content" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.3" stroke-width="1.5">
              <line x1="212" y1="112" x2="228" y2="112" />
              <line x1="404" y1="112" x2="420" y2="112" />
              <line x1="596" y1="112" x2="612" y2="112" />
              <line x1="788" y1="112" x2="804" y2="112" />
            </g>

            <g :for={
              {label, note, who, x} <- [
                {"Plan", "what you want built", "you", 40},
                {"Spec", "what it should do", "you", 232},
                {"Code", "it builds until the tests pass", "it", 424},
                {"QA", "it uses the app like a customer", "it", 616},
                {"Review", "you say if it is right", "you", 808}
              ]
            }>
              <g class={if who == "you", do: "text-primary", else: "text-base-content"}>
                <rect
                  x={x}
                  y="74"
                  width="176"
                  height="76"
                  rx="10"
                  fill="currentColor"
                  fill-opacity={if who == "you", do: "0.09", else: "0.03"}
                  stroke="currentColor"
                  stroke-opacity={if who == "you", do: "0.85", else: "0.25"}
                  stroke-width={if who == "you", do: "1.75", else: "1"}
                />
                <text
                  x={x + 88}
                  y="58"
                  text-anchor="middle"
                  font-size="12"
                  font-weight="700"
                  letter-spacing="1.5"
                  fill="currentColor"
                  fill-opacity={if who == "you", do: "0.9", else: "0.45"}
                >
                  {String.upcase(who)}
                </text>
                <text x={x + 88} y="108" text-anchor="middle" font-size="21" font-weight="700" fill="currentColor">
                  {label}
                </text>
              </g>
              <text
                x={x + 88}
                y="132"
                text-anchor="middle"
                font-size="11.5"
                class="text-base-content"
                fill="currentColor"
                fill-opacity="0.6"
              >
                {note}
              </text>
            </g>

            <%!-- what QA finds never reaches you: it goes back to Code --%>
            <g class="text-base-content" stroke="currentColor" stroke-opacity="0.45" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4">
              <path d="M 704 150 L 704 196 L 512 196 L 512 150" />
            </g>
            <g class="text-base-content">
              <path d="M 512 150 l -4 8 l 8 0 z" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.45" stroke="none" />
              <text x="608" y="216" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.6">
                anything QA finds goes back before you see it
              </text>
            </g>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/60 mt-3 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed">
          You never write the code and you never prompt the model. You answer questions at the start
          and you judge the result at the end.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- ───────────── ARCHITECTURE (diagram) — the no-lock-in proof ───────────── --%>
  <section class="border-b border-base-300">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <.eyebrow name="no lock-in" />
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        It doesn't sit between you and the model. It sits around it.
      </h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-base-content/70 mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-base-content">
          The harness is a local server on your machine, and each plugin is a thin adapter that
          points your agent at it.
        </strong>
        Your requests to the model are never proxied, metered, or marked up. That is also why
        supporting a new agent is a small adapter rather than a rewrite.
      </p>

      <figure class="mt-12">
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-base-300 bg-base-100">
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 1040 300"
            class="w-full h-auto min-w-[820px]"
            role="img"
            aria-labelledby="arch-title arch-desc"
          >
            <title id="arch-title">Where CodeMySpec sits in your toolchain</title>
            <desc id="arch-desc">
              Your coding agent talks directly to your model provider using your own keys, and
              CodeMySpec is not in that path. Separately, a thin plugin adapter connects the agent to
              the cms server running on your machine, which reads and writes the executable
              specifications and tests in your own repository.
            </desc>

            <%!-- the model path: direct, and we are not on it --%>
            <g class="text-base-content">
              <rect
                x="40"
                y="40"
                width="200"
                height="64"
                rx="8"
                fill="currentColor"
                fill-opacity="0.03"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-opacity="0.25"
              />
              <text x="140" y="66" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="700" fill="currentColor">
                Your coding agent
              </text>
              <text x="140" y="86" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55">
                Claude Code · Codex · Antigravity
              </text>

              <rect
                x="800"
                y="40"
                width="200"
                height="64"
                rx="8"
                fill="currentColor"
                fill-opacity="0.03"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-opacity="0.25"
              />
              <text x="900" y="66" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="700" fill="currentColor">
                Your model provider
              </text>
              <text x="900" y="86" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55">
                your keys · your bill
              </text>

              <line
                x1="240"
                y1="72"
                x2="792"
                y2="72"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-opacity="0.35"
                stroke-width="1.5"
              />
              <path d="M 800 72 l -8 -4 l 0 8 z" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.35" />
              <text x="520" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.6">
                direct — we are not in this path
              </text>
            </g>

            <%!-- the harness path --%>
            <g class="text-primary">
              <line
                x1="140"
                y1="104"
                x2="140"
                y2="168"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="1.5"
              />
              <path d="M 140 176 l -4 -8 l 8 0 z" fill="currentColor" />
              <text x="152" y="140" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill="currentColor">
                plugin — a thin adapter
              </text>

              <rect
                x="40"
                y="176"
                width="200"
                height="72"
                rx="8"
                fill="currentColor"
                fill-opacity="0.08"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="1.5"
              />
              <text x="140" y="204" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="700" fill="currentColor">
                cms — local server
              </text>
              <text x="140" y="222" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.8">
                requirement graph · specs
              </text>
              <text x="140" y="238" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.8">
                verification · QA
              </text>
            </g>

            <g class="text-base-content">
              <line
                x1="240"
                y1="212"
                x2="432"
                y2="212"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-opacity="0.35"
                stroke-width="1.5"
              />
              <path d="M 440 212 l -8 -4 l 0 8 z" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.35" />

              <rect
                x="440"
                y="176"
                width="240"
                height="72"
                rx="8"
                fill="currentColor"
                fill-opacity="0.03"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-opacity="0.25"
              />
              <text x="560" y="204" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="700" fill="currentColor">
                Your repository
              </text>
              <text x="560" y="222" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55">
                your specs · your tests
              </text>
              <text x="560" y="238" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.55">
                your code, on your disk
              </text>
            </g>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <figcaption class="text-sm text-base-content/60 mt-3 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed">
          Everything of value stays on your side of the line. Stop using CodeMySpec and you keep the
          executable specifications, the tests, and a standard app that compiles and ships without us.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- ───────────── QA — with the proof attached ───────────── --%>
  <section class="border-b border-base-300">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <.eyebrow name="proving it works" />
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        "Tests pass" is not the same as "it works."
      </h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-base-content/70 mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-base-content">
          A QA agent opens your running application in a real browser and uses it — story by story,
          then all the way through, the way a customer would.
        </strong>
        That catches the things tests never see: the flow that breaks at the seams, the screen that
        looks right in the code and wrong to a person.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6">
        <.link navigate={~p"/blog/agentic-qa"} class="link link-primary font-medium">
          Read what browser QA found on a real product, including a fraud surface the BDD specs
          marked as passing →
        </.link>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- ───────────── PRICING ───────────── --%>
  <section class="border-b border-base-300 bg-base-200">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <.eyebrow name="what it costs" />
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        Building is free. You pay when it's running.
      </h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-base-content/70 mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-base-content">
          Building costs nothing, and that is not a trial.
        </strong>
        The hosted layer for running and growing the software afterwards — customer email on your own
        domain, live chat, content publishing, working as a team — is $100 per month per user. Having it
        built with you starts at $500, quoted. You never pay us for AI usage.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base-content/70 mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        Here is why that split matters, and it is the honest reason to trust this over the alternative.
        A tool that charges you to build has a reason to keep you building: close enough to the answer
        to keep paying, never close enough to walk away with a finished thing.
        <span class="font-semibold text-base-content">
          Their goal is to keep you subscribed. Your goal is to end up owning something that makes you
          money or saves you time.
        </span>
        We only get paid once your software is live and earning, so getting you out the door with a
        working application is the entire business.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6">
        <.link navigate={~p"/pricing"} class="link link-primary font-medium">See full pricing →</.link>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- ───────────── FAQ — same list drives the FAQPage schema ───────────── --%>
  <section class="border-b border-base-300">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <.eyebrow name="questions" />
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mt-3">Straight answers.</h2>
      <.faq faqs={@faqs} name="product-faq" class="mt-10 max-w-3xl" />

      <%!-- This page is the hub, so it feeds the feature pages rather than
           sending readers back into the blog. Feature links come first and get
           the weight; comparisons stay because someone in the FAQ is evaluating,
           but they sit second and quieter. --%>
      <div class="mt-12 max-w-3xl">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-primary">Go deeper</h3>
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 mt-4">
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/design-system"}
            class="card bg-base-100 border border-base-300 hover:border-primary transition-colors"
          >
            <div class="card-body py-4">
              <span class="font-semibold">Design system →</span>
              <span class="text-sm text-base-content/60">
                Decide how it looks once, so every page stops guessing.
              </span>
            </div>
          </.link>
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/agents"}
            class="card bg-base-100 border border-base-300 hover:border-primary transition-colors"
          >
            <div class="card-body py-4">
              <span class="font-semibold">Supported agents →</span>
              <span class="text-sm text-base-content/60">
                Claude Code, Codex and Antigravity, and why all three work.
              </span>
            </div>
          </.link>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-sm text-base-content/60">
          <.link navigate={~p"/blog/lovable-alternatives"} class="link link-hover">
            Compared with Lovable
          </.link>
          <.link navigate={~p"/blog/codemyspec-vs-kiro-specs"} class="link link-hover">
            Compared with Kiro
          </.link>
          <.link navigate={~p"/developers"} class="link link-hover">
            The technical detail, for engineers
          </.link>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- ───────────── FINAL CTA ───────────── --%>
  <section class="bg-base-200">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-20 lg:py-28 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        Try it on one real feature. <span class="text-primary">Building is free.</span>
      </h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-base-content/70 mt-5 max-w-xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
        Install the plugin, run <code class="text-base-content">/init</code> to link your project,
        then <code class="text-base-content">/next</code> to start the next requirement.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4 justify-center">
        <.link navigate={~p"/install"} class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Install the plugin — free</.link>
        <.link navigate={~p"/methodology"} class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg">Read the methodology →</.link>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layouts.marketing>
